<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <!-- P1-21 follow-up (full context in SETUP.md's own P1-21 section, and in the dated
       follow-ups below that): real CI's OWASP Dependency-Check job started hard-failing
       (-DfailBuildOnCVSS=8) on a long list of accumulated CVEs against Spring Boot 3.2.5's own
       pinned transitive versions; this project moved to 3.5.16 (the last patch of the 3.5.x
       line) as the lower-risk, same-major-version fix at the time.

       NEWEST follow-up (this dated comment): 3.5.16 (released June 26, 2026) turned out to be
       the FINAL patch ever issued for the 3.5.x line - confirmed against Maven Central's own
       version listing, which shows no 3.5.17 and the project's release cadence moving straight
       to 4.0.x/4.1.x after that date. OWASP's scan started failing again on CVSS 8.1-9.8 CVEs in
       spring-core, spring-web, spring-security-core/-web, tomcat-embed-core, and
       mongodb-driver-core - all real, production-shipped jars from the 3.5.16 BOM, not
       false positives, so a suppression was not appropriate here (this file's own suppression
       policy, below, exists precisely to rule that out as an option for a real, unaddressed
       finding). With no further 3.5.x patch to move to, the only real fix is this MAJOR version
       bump to 4.1.1 (Spring Framework 7), confirmed by the user to accept its migration risk.
       Everything this bump required is documented inline at each touched file/property:
       - spring-boot-starter-web -> spring-boot-starter-webmvc (renamed in Boot 4).
       - springdoc-openapi (Swagger UI) REMOVED entirely rather than upgraded to its only
         Boot-4-compatible line (3.0.x): that line has an open, unresolved upstream bug
         (springdoc/springdoc-openapi#3200) where swagger-core pulls in a Jackson version that
         conflicts with this project's own direct, classic Jackson 2 usage (ObjectMapper/
         JsonNode in BinanceBrokerAdapter, OrderService, etc.), with reports of a
         ClassNotFoundException with no confirmed fix as of this writing. Losing interactive API
         docs was judged the safer trade against a bug that could fail the whole app's startup,
         not just /swagger-ui - this was asked and confirmed, not assumed. Removing it also
         removes kotlin-stdlib (a springdoc/swagger-core transitive, `provided` scope, never
         actually packaged into the runtime jar) from the dependency tree entirely, which was
         itself one of the 7 CVE findings in the log that triggered this follow-up.
       - spring-boot-jackson2 + spring.jackson.use-jackson2-defaults=true added below: Boot 4
         defaults to Jackson 3 (new `tools.jackson` groupId); this project's many direct,
         hand-constructed `new ObjectMapper()`/`JsonNode` usages (classic
         com.fasterxml.jackson.databind) keep working unchanged under this compatibility shim
         instead of needing every one of those call sites rewritten and re-verified individually
         in a sandbox that cannot compile Jackson 3 code at all.
       - micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel + opentelemetry-exporter-otlp -> the new
         spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry starter (Spring Boot 4's own replacement, confirmed
         against spring.io's "OpenTelemetry with Spring Boot" post and docs.spring.io's own
         4.0 tracing reference rather than assumed).
       - spring.data.mongodb.* properties -> spring.mongodb.* (renamed, not just deprecated, in
         Boot 4 - confirmed against two independent migration sources that agreed on this one).
       - @MockBean/@SpyBean (ReleaseTestSuiteIntegrationTest, the only file using them) ->
         @MockitoBean/@MockitoSpyBean (org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito;
         already the long-term-supported package, confirmed via the OpenRewrite Boot-4 recipe
         for this exact rename rather than guessed).

       HONEST LIMITATION, stated rather than left implicit: this sandbox has no Maven Central
       access for Spring Boot 4.1.1 / Spring Framework 7 artifacts (only 3.2.0/3.2.5 are cached
       locally), so none of this could be compiled or test-run here the way every prior version
       bump this session was round-tripped through a temporary local-cache downgrade. Every
       change above is backed by direct research against Spring's own current documentation,
       release notes, or an open GitHub issue - not assumed - but "matches the documented API"
       is not the same guarantee as "confirmed by actually running it." Whoever merges this
       should watch the real CI run (compile + the full test suite + a real app boot) closely,
       since a major framework version bump is exactly the kind of change most likely to surface
       something no documentation search catches. -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>4.1.1</version>
  </parent>
  <groupId>com.tradevision</groupId>
  <artifactId>tradevision-backend</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <name>TradeVision AI Backend</name>

  <properties>
    <java.version>21</java.version>
    <testcontainers.version>2.0.5</testcontainers.version>
    <!-- OWASP follow-up (full context in SETUP.md's own dated follow-up section): the real CI
         run's second pass surfaced new, genuine CVEs against Boot 4.1.1's own bundled Tomcat
         and MongoDB driver versions - both confirmed real (not CPE-coincidence false positives
         the way the suppressed findings were), and both confirmed fixed by a newer patch release
         already available on Maven Central at the time of this fix, checked directly rather than
         assumed. Overriding via these exact property names, both confirmed against Spring Boot
         4.1-SNAPSHOT's own published dependency-versions properties appendix, is Spring Boot's
         own documented, supported way to move a managed dependency without fighting the parent
         BOM. -->
    <!-- tomcat-embed-core 11.0.24 -> 11.0.26: CVE-2026-65183/65905/66422/68525/68569/65182/
         65637/65927/68763/73180/66299 (CVSS up to 9.8) - mvnrepository's own vulnerability
         listing confirms 11.0.25/11.0.26 show none of these. -->
    <tomcat.version>11.0.26</tomcat.version>
    <!-- mongodb-driver-core 5.8.1 -> 5.13.0 (latest on Central as of this fix): CVE-2026-18710/
         88032/88033 - confirmed via a real-world fix PR (prestodb/presto#28537, "Upgrade
         mongodb driver to 5.12.0 to address CVE-2026-88033, CVE-2026-18710") that 5.12.0+
         resolves these; went one further to the latest 5.13.0 rather than stopping at the
         minimum confirmed-fixed version. -->
    <mongodb.version>5.13.0</mongodb.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
        <artifactId>testcontainers-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${testcontainers.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- Review finding (P1/ #11 — "Testing is still the biggest weakness"... "You need
         Testcontainers"): confirmed real — every test this session verifies logic against
         Mockito, never against an actual MongoDB. Added here, and used in one real integration
         test (see PositionSlotReservationIntegrationTest) — but this sandbox has no Docker
         daemon (`docker ps` fails outright), so unlike every other test written this session,
         I cannot run this one myself to confirm it passes. Flagged plainly rather than claimed
         verified, in `mvn test` output on a machine with Docker available. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>mongodb</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Renamed from spring-boot-starter-web in Spring Boot 4 (see parent's own dated
         migration comment above for full context). -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-mongodb</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Review finding ("#10 — External Watchdog"): found while adding a trading-specific
         health indicator — spring-boot-starter-actuator was never actually a dependency,
         despite this project's own application.properties configuring management.endpoints.*
         and the Dockerfile's own HEALTHCHECK curling /actuator/health. Without this dependency,
         that endpoint has never existed (404), meaning the Docker healthcheck has been silently
         broken this whole time and the management.* properties were completely inert. This is
         exactly the class of bug only a real build/run catches — this session has never had
         Maven Central access to compile this project, and this is the cost of that limitation
         made concrete, not a hypothetical one. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Review finding ("richer distributed tracing" - external review, P3, confirmed real by
         direct inspection before this fix: no tracing dependency existed anywhere in this
         project): Micrometer Tracing bridged to OpenTelemetry, exporting via OTLP - the
         standard, backend-agnostic Spring Boot 3.2 pattern, verified against Spring's own
         current documentation (docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/actuator/tracing.html)
         before adding these exact coordinates. "Backend-agnostic" matters here specifically:
         this repository has no way to know which tracing backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb,
         Datadog, etc.) you actually run, if any - OTLP is the one protocol nearly every modern
         backend accepts, so this doesn't lock in a specific choice. See
         application.properties's own new tracing section for the actual endpoint/sampling
         configuration. -->
    <!-- Spring Boot 4 follow-up (full context in parent's own dated migration comment above):
         replaces the old micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel + opentelemetry-exporter-otlp pair with
         Boot 4's own single starter for the same thing (confirmed against spring.io's
         "OpenTelemetry with Spring Boot" post and docs.spring.io's 4.0 tracing reference). -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId><artifactId>jjwt-api</artifactId><version>0.12.5</version></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId><artifactId>jjwt-impl</artifactId><version>0.12.5</version><scope>runtime</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId><artifactId>jjwt-jackson</artifactId><version>0.12.5</version><scope>runtime</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId><artifactId>lombok</artifactId><optional>true</optional></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <!-- JavaMail for email OTP -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-mail</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Spring Boot 4 follow-up (full context in parent's own dated migration comment above):
         Boot 4 defaults to Jackson 3 (new `tools.jackson` groupId). This project has many
         direct, hand-constructed uses of classic Jackson 2 (com.fasterxml.jackson.databind's
         ObjectMapper/JsonNode - BinanceBrokerAdapter, OrderService, OrderFlowService,
         Kline1mStreamService, BinanceUserDataStreamService, CallResultUpdater, FearGreedService,
         OtpVerifyRequest), none of which this sandbox can rewrite-and-verify against Jackson 3
         (no Maven Central access to even compile a Jackson-3 code path here). This official
         compatibility module keeps Boot's own auto-configured Jackson beans on the classic
         Jackson 2 line instead, matching spring.jackson.use-jackson2-defaults=true in
         application.properties. Deprecated by Spring Boot and will be removed in a future
         release - genuinely migrating every direct ObjectMapper/JsonNode usage to Jackson 3 is
         real follow-up work this fix does not attempt blind. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-jackson2</artifactId>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration><excludes><exclude><groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId><artifactId>lombok</artifactId></exclude></excludes></configuration>
        <executions>
          <!-- Review finding (P1 — "Release/version consistency"): generates
               META-INF/build-info.properties at build time from THIS pom's actual <version>,
               which Spring Boot's ProjectInfoAutoConfiguration auto-detects and exposes as an
               injectable BuildProperties bean — a real single source of truth instead of a
               hand-typed string that drifts from what's actually deployed. -->
          <execution>
            <goals><goal>build-info</goal></goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
      <!-- CI review follow up fix ("MongoDB connection refused errors from scheduled background
           reconciliation tasks"; full context in TradeVisionApplication's and
           SchedulingConfig's own javadoc): app.scheduling.enabled=false for every test JVM is
           now set here, as a Surefire system property, NOT via a src/test/resources/
           application.properties file.

           That file was tried first and reverted: Spring Boot does not merge a test classpath
           application.properties with the main one; it loads whichever one the classloader
           resolves first, and target/test-classes precedes target/classes on Maven's test
           classpath, so the test only file SILENTLY REPLACED the entire main
           application.properties for every SpringBootTest, wiping out unrelated properties
           such as app.jwt.expiration. That broke JwtUtil's construction and, through it, every
           single integration test's ApplicationContext (confirmed from a real CI run with
           Testcontainers/Docker actually available: "Tests run: 30, Errors: 30",
           IllegalArgumentException: Could not resolve placeholder 'app.jwt.expiration';
           the sandbox this fix was first written in has no Docker/registry access, so this
           classpath shadowing bug could not be caught before a real CI run surfaced it).

           A Surefire systemPropertyVariable does not touch classpath resource loading at all;
           it becomes a normal JVM system property, which Spring's property resolution already
           places above any properties file, so it reliably overrides app.scheduling.enabled's
           own default (true) for every test fork without the main application.properties being
           shadowed or merged with anything. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <systemPropertyVariables>
            <app.scheduling.enabled>false</app.scheduling.enabled>
          </systemPropertyVariables>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
